==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|wynsum||winsome, pleasant, joyful, merry|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{recons|wunisamaz||joyful|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{recons|wunjō||joy, delight, desire|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|wun-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|wenǝ-||to wish, love|lang=ine-pro}}, equivalent to {{suffix|winne|some}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|winsome|lang=sco}}, {{term|wunsome||charming, comely, pleasing|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{term|wunnesam||winsome, joyful, delightful|lang=gmh}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|wynn||joy, rapture, pleasure, delight|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Wonne||bliss, delight, joy|lang=de}}. More at {{l|en|winne}}, {{l|en|winly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈwɪn.sʌm/}} {{X-SAMPA|/"wIn.sVm/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[charming|Charming]]; inspiring [[trust]] and [[approval]]; especially if in an innocent manner.
#: ''His bedside manner was especially '''winsome'''.''
#* '''1922''', {{w|James Joyce}}, ''[[w:Ulysses (novel)|Ulysses]]'' Chapter 13
#*: Gerty MacDowell who was seated near her companions, lost in thought, gazing far away into the distance was, in very truth, as fair a specimen of '''winsome''' Irish girlhood as one could wish to see.

